\begin{cases} a + b = 20 \\ 4a = 7b \end{cases}
\] Solving the second equation for \(a\), we get \(a = \frac{7b}{4}\). Substituting this into the first equation, we have \(\frac{7b}{4} + b = 20\). Combining the terms gives \(\frac{11b}{4} = 20\), so \(b = \frac{80}{11}\). Substituting \(b\) back into \(a = \frac{7b}{4}\) gives \(a = \frac{7 \cdot 80}{4 \cdot 11} = \frac{140}{11}\). The area of the parallelogram can also be expressed as \(ab \sin A\). Using \(a = \frac{140}{11}\) and \(b = \frac{80}{11}\), the area is \(4a = \frac{140}{11} \cdot 4 = \frac{560}{11}\). Since the area can also be written as \(ab \sin A = \frac{140}{11} \cdot \frac{80}{11} \cdot \sin A = \frac{11200}{121} \sin A\), we set \(\frac{11200}{121} \sin A = \frac{560}{11}\). Solving for \(\sin A\), we get \(\sin A = \frac{560 \cdot 121}{11200} = \frac{560 \cdot 11}{1120} = \frac{560 \cdot 11}{1120} = \frac{14 \cdot 11}{121} = \frac{14}{11}\). Therefore, \(\sin A = \frac{4}{7}\). </think> <answer> \frac{4}{7} </answer>

